
From scaling to cognitive inheritance in artificial intelligence

Artificial intelligence has advanced by scale. Larger neural networks trained on broader datasets with greater computational budgets have delivered remarkable gains in language, vision, coding, scientific reasoning, and multimodal inference. But this success conceals a growing inefficiency. Each new generation of frontier models is trained as if much of mature human knowledge must be learned again. Mathematical formalisms, physical regularities, biological constraints, engineering heuristics, and stable structures of symbolic and social reasoning are repeatedly reabsorbed through another civilization-scale optimization cycle. The result is powerful, but also energetically expensive, organizationally centralized, and developmentally shallow.

Biological intelligence offers a different template. Brains are not rebuilt from raw sensory data in every generation. They inherit developmental biases, adaptive priors, instinctive procedures, and plastic architectures that shape learning before experience begins. Natural intelligence therefore does not start from epistemic emptiness. It starts from inherited structure, incorporates new experience selectively, prunes ineffective pathways, and grows increasingly general cognition from layered prior competence. Intelligence in nature is not repeatedly reconstructed. It is inherited, refined, recombined, and developed.

This contrast suggests a complementary path for AI. Rather than treating each frontier system as a near-total retraining of civilization’s accumulated knowledge, one can ask whether machine intelligence could be built through cognitive inheritance: the transfer, compression, integration, and renewal of reusable competence acquired elsewhere. In this view, general intelligence would emerge not only from scaling homogeneous models, but from combining inherited domain expertise with developmental plasticity in an architecture able to reorganize itself over time. I refer to this broader framework as Cognitive Seeded Neural Morphogenesis.

At its foundation is what may be called civilizational procedural memory. Human civilization contains far more than declarative knowledge. Embedded in scientific practice, engineering design, medical diagnosis, mathematical proof, legal interpretation, and artistic production are compact procedural regularities: causal intuitions, recurrent reasoning motifs, robust heuristics, and efficient inferential habits repeatedly selected by contact with reality. These are not merely facts stored in texts or datasets. They are forms of operational cognition. A useful AI inheritance regime would need to capture this procedural layer, not just its surface expression.

Such compression must preserve at least three kinds of structure. The first is causal structure: not just correlations, but the generative relations by which systems produce outcomes. The second is developmental structure: the historical pathways through which concepts emerged, including failed hypotheses, intermediate abstractions, and competing frameworks. Innovation often depends on understanding how ideas changed, not only on accessing their polished final form. The third is procedural structure: the domain-specific ways of thinking that make expertise efficient, such as mathematical reflex, diagnostic triage, experimental design, or engineering approximation.

These compressed inheritances can be conceptualized as crystallized epistemic instincts: compact, reusable units of procedural competence. A crystallized epistemic instinct is not simply a frozen expert model, and not only a distilled specialist network. It is a transferable inheritance unit that preserves a coherent mode of reasoning within a domain. Such units could arise directly from compressed civilizational memory, or indirectly from specialist AI systems developed across the wider research ecosystem and then selectively distilled for reuse.

This distinction changes the economics of intelligence. Under the prevailing paradigm, each new general model must re-acquire substantial competence in mathematics, chemistry, biology, law, robotics, and other fields through another unified training cycle. But specialist cognition is already being generated in parallel—across universities, industrial laboratories, open-source communities, and domain-specific engineering groups. If the best of these systems could be competitively evaluated, compressed into interoperable inheritance units, and incorporated into larger architectures, then expertise would become at least partly cumulative. Intelligence would no longer need to be rebuilt wholesale; it could be inherited.

Why might this matter now? Because the conditions that made scale alone such a productive strategy are changing. Training costs and energy demands are rising sharply. Performance gains from pretraining alone are becoming harder to separate from gains delivered by data curation, post-training, tool use, retrieval, and specialized workflows. At the same time, the AI ecosystem is fragmenting into a vast landscape of narrow but highly capable systems: models for protein structure, theorem assistance, code synthesis, robotic control, materials design, and domain-specific reasoning. The field now faces a coordination problem, not only a scaling problem. The central question is increasingly how to make specialist cognition composable, updateable, and cumulative.

A developmental architecture for such composition should mirror a basic property of knowledge itself: its hierarchical and branching organization. Physics divides into mechanics, thermodynamics, quantum theory, and narrower subfields; biology divides into genetics, cell signaling, immunology, and ecology; mathematics branches recursively across abstraction levels and methods. Human knowledge is not flat. It is tree-like and self-similar across scales. AI systems that aim to inherit civilization’s competence may need to reflect that structure.

In a seeded morphogenetic framework, narrow specialist systems would occupy the leaves of this cognitive tree. Through evaluation and compression, they would be transformed into crystallized epistemic instincts. Related units could then be fused into broader domain-level assemblies, which could themselves be integrated into still larger structures. Recursive aggregation would allow complexity to be managed locally while making higher-order integration more tractable. Instead of forcing one monolithic model to internalize everything at once, the system would grow upward from increasingly mature, testable, and interoperable components.

But modularity alone is insufficient. A bag of experts is not general intelligence. The crucial requirement is a trainable substrate that allows inherited competencies to interact, compete, and reorganize. One can think of this substrate as a plastic morphogenetic field: a broadly learnable representational medium in which different inherited units begin with weak potential couplings that are strengthened, weakened, or pruned through use. Repeatedly useful interactions become stable pathways. Persistently low-value interactions fade. Novel bridges emerge where cross-domain dependence proves functionally important. Learning in such a system changes not only parameters but effective topology.

The mature result would not be a routed collection of sealed modules, nor a fully homogeneous latent space. It would be a continuous cognitive manifold: a structured but fluid cognitive tissue in which some regions preserve strong domain-specific procedural identity while others become zones of overlap, transfer, and hybridization. These mixed regions may be especially important. Many of the hallmarks of general intelligence—analogy, abstraction, conceptual transfer, and creative synthesis—appear most strongly at the boundaries between fields rather than within them. A developmental architecture should therefore preserve distinction without enforcing isolation.

A further advantage of cognitive inheritance is updateability. Today’s large models are often treated as disposable wholes: major new capability frequently requires large retraining runs or costly post hoc adaptation. In a morphogenetic architecture, by contrast, parts of the system could be selectively renewed. New inheritance units could be inserted; obsolete ones could be replaced; weak ones could be pruned; better specialist systems could be incorporated later. Limited reintegration within the plastic substrate could restore coherence while preserving most of the system’s stable competence. In practical terms, this would make intelligence renewable rather than repeatedly rebuilt.

The argument here is not that scaling has ended, nor that inherited architectures will automatically outperform large pretrained models. Scale remains a powerful engine of representation learning. But scale alone may be a poor long-term theory of cumulative intelligence. A civilization does not progress by teaching every individual all knowledge from scratch. It progresses by encoding hard-won competence into reusable forms—in education, institutions, tools, scientific methods, and technological platforms—and then building further on that inheritance. If AI is to become more capable, efficient, and widely participatory, it may require an analogous shift from repeated absorption to structured inheritance.

This proposal also defines an experimental agenda. Can procedural structure be extracted from scientific corpora and expert workflows rather than from text statistics alone? Can specialist systems be distilled into transferable inheritance units that preserve causal and procedural competence? What kinds of plastic substrate best support integration without catastrophic interference? Does hierarchical aggregation improve sample efficiency, robustness, or update cost? Can selective renewal preserve old competence while incorporating genuinely new reasoning structures? These are engineering questions, not only metaphors, and they are increasingly tractable.

The strategic implication is broader than architecture. If frontier AI remains tied mainly to ever-larger centralized training runs, progress will remain coupled to concentrated compute, capital intensity, and repeated energetic expenditure. If intelligence can also be built through inheritance, recombination, and selective renewal, then progress may arise from distributed specialization and cumulative cognitive infrastructure. The key challenge would no longer be only how to train the next largest model. It would be how to construct reusable cognitive heredity for machine intelligence.

Artificial intelligence may therefore be approaching a transition. The next leap may not come solely from making models larger, but from enabling them to grow more like evolving knowledge systems: seeded by inherited competence, shaped by developmental plasticity, and renewed through the selective integration of new cognitive structures.
